n. A genuinely escrowed ERC-721 with a sponsor-controlled transfer-pause role therefore passes the creation-time safeTransferFrom, receiver callback, ownerOf, and Active-status checks while unpaused. After entries are sold, the sponsor can pause transfers. With reserveEntries set to 1, any nonempty raffle resolves NftWon. Permissionless settlement then credits 95% of the pot to sponsorProceeds without delivering the NFT, and releaseSponsorProceeds transfers those funds without requiring winnerRedeemed or prizeClaimed. The winner's transferFrom subsequently reverts while the token is paused, and enableRefunds rejects the terminal NftWon state. Thus the sponsor receives the sale proceeds while entrants can obtain neither the advertised NFT nor refunds."},{"id":2,"summary":"A predictable future clone address bypasses the snapshot-only sponsor-recipient registry check, allowing permanent cross-raffle asset lockup.","vulnerability_type":"Improper input validation / predictable future-address TOCTOU bypass","file_path":"packages/contracts/src/RaffleFactory.sol","line":166,"exploitable":true,"malicious_actor":"A sponsor-controlled EOA or helper contract that owns and approves two ERC-721 prizes","trigger_flow":["packages/contracts/src/RaffleFactory.sol:97 receives a sponsorRecipient equal to the predictable address B of a later factory clone.","packages/contracts/src/RaffleFactory.sol:163-168 accepts B because it is not yet deployed or marked in isRaffle.","packages/contracts/src/RaffleFactory.sol:105-121 deploys and initializes raffle A; packages/contracts/src/Raffle.sol:193 and 598-604 also accept the code-less, unregistered B.","A second RaffleFactory.createRaffle call deploys B, and packages/contracts/src/RaffleFactory.sol:123-125 registers it as a raffle.","The sponsor calls packages/contracts/src/Raffle.sol:276-300 to put empty raffle A into Refunding.","Anyone calls packages/contracts/src/Raffle.sol:395-405, which transfers A's configured prize into registered raffle B with no recovery path."],"explanation":"The validation only rejects addresses already present in `isRaffle`. Because `Clones.clone` uses CREATE and the factory creates exactly one clone per successful call, future clone addresses are predictable. A sponsor can configure raffle A with the still-code-less address of future raffle B; both the factory check and `Raffle.initialize` accept it. After B is created and registered, A's immutable recipient has become protocol plumbing. In `Refunding` or `CashWon`, `releaseSponsorPrize` transfers A's NFT into B using plain `transferFrom`, where it is permanently stranded because B has no arbitrary-NFT rescue path. If A instead has sponsor proceeds, their release permanently reverts because the runtime quote-transfer guard now recognizes B as a registered raffle. The direct financial loss is confined to the sponsor-selected prize or proceeds, but the bypass concretely defeats the destination safeguard and permits assets to move between canonical raffle clones."},{"id":3,"summary":"An attacker-controlled ERC-721 can forge the ownership attestation and create an Active, canonically registered raffle without an enforceable prize escrow.","vulnerability_type":"Improper validation of an untrusted ERC-721 leading to forged escrow attestation","file_path":"packages/contracts/src/RaffleFactory.sol","line":129,"exploitable":true,"malicious_actor":"A malicious raffle sponsor who controls the supplied prize-token implementation","trigger_flow":["A malicious sponsor deploys or controls an ERC-721-like contract and calls `RaffleFactory.createRaffle` with it as `params.prizeToken` (`RaffleFactory.sol:97`).","The token passes code-presence and self-reported ERC-165 checks (`RaffleFactory.sol:155-177`).","The factory initializes and registers the clone before dispatching `safeTransferFrom` (`RaffleFactory.sol:105-128`).","During `safeTransferFrom`, the malicious token calls `Raffle.onERC721Received` with the factory as operator and matching